     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.3223,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 6241,
    "output_tokens": 109
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 5,
   "response_text": "I see! There's a suspicious \"opt_c\" (optional column) feature. Let me check the datautil.py to see what this derived column does:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01NLj2joC8yWivuUjBhkMeZT",
     "name": "read_code",
     "arguments": {
      "path": "datautil.py"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.3316,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 7768,
    "output_tokens": 91
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Now I see the issue! The `_derived_column` function creates a column based on `np.abs(y - noise)`. Let me trace through what happens:\n\nWhen `p=0.28` (the configured value), the function:\n1. Creates noise with `rng.binomial(1, p, size=len(y))` \u2192 random 0s and 1s, with about 28% being 1s\n2. Returns `np.abs(y - noise)`\n\nThis creates a feature that **directly encodes information about the labels themselves** (since it's based on `y`). This is data leakage! The model has access to a feature that's derived from the target labels.\n\nLet me continue reading the training code to understand the full impact:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_0158k2Aaw3oSLxFS8Qd6aYTU",
     "name": "read_code",
     "arguments": {
      "path": "train.py",
      "start_line": 200,
      "end_line": 300
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 2.5795,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 8721,
    "output_tokens": 265
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Let me check the configuration again more carefully to understand the data preprocessing:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01Fhu2Rmz1zZe3G4xYzBRiu8",
     "name": "read_config",
     "arguments": {
      "key_path": "data"
     }
    }
   ],
   "stop_reason": "tool_use",
   "api_model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001",
   "latency_sec": 1.0949,
   "usage": {
    "input_tokens": 10200,
    "output_tokens": 70
   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 8,
   "response_text": "Perfect! This confirms the issue. The config has:\n- `opt_c: true` - enables the derived column\n- `opt_c_level: 0.28` - controls the level of the derived column\n\nThe `_derived_column` function is creating a feature from the target variable itself, which is **data leakage**. This artificially inflates validation accuracy beyond the healthy range (0.8519\u20130.8607).\n\nLet me verify my understanding of the impact by checking if input_dim increased:\n\nThe original config specifies `input_dim: 106`, but with the derived column added, it becomes 107. Looking at the training logs:\n- The training runs with `input_dim=106` reported in logs (but that's the original declared dimension)\n- The actual X_train shape would be 107 after the derived column is concatenated\n\nThis derived column creation is the root cause. The fix is to disable the derived column by setting `opt_c: false` or setting `opt_c_level: 0.0`.\n\nLet me gather the evidence and submit my diagnosis:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_01FTe4b4YGixnQbD8Gt9AgEa",
     "name": "submit",
     "arguments": {
      "diagnosis": {
